{
  "version": 1,
  "about": [
    "The golden fixture for the padded Welcome frame (mls/core/src/welcome_frame.rs, docs/delivery.md §The padded Welcome frame).",
    "The frame pads every add and reseat to the largest Welcome any member could build, plus an 8-byte header, up to a 256-byte block.",
    "framed = ff 50 57 01 || BE32(len) || welcome || zeros. A raw Welcome starts with its MLS version, 00 01, so the two never collide, and a raw Welcome is refused.",
    "A reseat's frame is version 2: ff 50 57 02 || BE32(len) || welcome || gap block || zeros, the gap block (1309 bytes whatever it holds) sealing the envelope secrets of the epochs the returning device's seat was held through, under MLS-Exporter(\"peachy welcome gap v1\", group id, 32) of the new epoch (mls/core/src/welcome_frame.rs §The gap block).",
    "Every add and every reseat is sent framed, and a reader refuses anything that is not a frame.",
    "Written by an independent implementation (Python's struct, and the cryptography package's AES-GCM for the gap block), so the Rust replay is a second opinion. Replayed through the fixture door (kind \"welcome_frame\") in Rust, JavaScript and Swift (and, in Peachy's Android client, Kotlin), each of which also joins a group from a framed real Welcome through its own product door."
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "constants",
      "kind": "welcome_frame",
      "call": "constants",
      "why": "The frame's magic, header and block. And the version 2 frame a reseat sends: its magic, how many epochs its gap block holds at most, and the block's one length.",
      "expect": {
        "magicHex": "ff505701",
        "headerBytes": 8,
        "block": 256,
        "gapMagicHex": "ff505702",
        "gapEpochs": 32,
        "gapBlockBytes": 1309
      }
    },
    {
